        <!-- RSI Deep Dive -->
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-indigo-950/20 border border-indigo-500/30 space-y-4 text-sm">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-bolt text-indigo-400"></i>
            <span>RSI, Again</span>
          </h4>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Lecture 1 already drew the line: RSI refers to a system using its own outputs to improve subsequent steps (prompts, tools, code, eval, or recipes). Bounded and layered self-improvement has been reported; a system that designs its own successor model autonomously has not.
          </p>
          <div class="border-t border-indigo-900/50 pt-3 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
            <p class="text-xs text-indigo-300 font-semibold">Public claims since September 2026 (all vendor/author self-reported, not third-party audits):</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs leading-relaxed list-disc list-inside pl-1 text-slate-300">
              <li><strong>Anthropic</strong> (internal tally to August 2026): Claude led ~26% of AI R&D tasks, collaborated in >90%, none fully autonomous. Anthropic defines full RSI as a model autonomously creating its successor, and states this has not occurred.</li>
              <li><strong>OpenAI</strong> (6 Sep 2026): claims reaching "automated research intern" milestone; targets "automated AI researcher" by March 2028. Chief Scientist discussed this timeline; automating training experiments is a separate track while humans set directions.</li>
              <li><strong>Dario Amodei</strong> (12 Sep 2026, "We Must Pace the Frontier"): argues for pacing frontier capability advances to allow time for alignment and verification.</li>
              <li><strong>Roadmap survey</strong> (arXiv:2609.11873, submitted Sep 10, revised Sep 22): categorizing RSI into execution, strategy, experience acquisition, environment adaptation, and recursive meta-improvement. This is a survey, not a live demo.</li>
              <li><strong>Weights fixed</strong> (Dream-RSI, arXiv:2609.14858, Sep 14; Google, DeepMind, etc.): using past search records as simulators to modify search procedures without updating model weights. Public notes indicate code not yet released.</li>
              <li><strong>Self-improving research agent</strong> (AIDE^2, arXiv:2609.26457, Sep 22): claiming 7 improvements across 8 days of autonomous run, author self-reported without independent replication.</li>
              <li><strong>Anti-overfitting regularized RSI</strong> (RRSI, arXiv:2609.24972, Chinese report Oct 6): modifying prompts, control flow, tools, and memory while regularizing against task overfitting. Author-reported scores.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p class="text-slate-400 text-xs border-t border-indigo-900/40 pt-2 italic">
            This lecture covers only these boundaries. Three-round song iteration remains an applied example. No model training demo.
          </p>
        </div>

